00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:24.220
Imagine you bought an ETF tracking Canadian consumer stocks last week when Aurezia guidance looked

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:30.900
constructive. Your order filled at the ask, but the bid asked spread on that ETF widened from

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:36.720
3 cents to 14 cents within the first 25 minutes of trading as retail volume spiked.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:43.260
What most retail investors don't realize is that market makers widen spreads precisely when order

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:51.060
flow becomes one-sided, so your effective cost can jump 0.4% even on a commission-free platform.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:57.420
The professionals always check level 2 data or the time and sales tape for the prior 15 minutes

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:01.420
before hitting market orders on anything below average daily volume.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:08.400
The biggest mistake with execution is assuming the quoted price is the price you'll get on fast moves.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:15.280
Instead, always use limit orders set one to two cents inside the current spread on ETFs or

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:21.820
liquid stocks when volatility exceeds the 20-day average. This is a simulated trade for educational

